  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nAnother pronounced trough with strong cyclonic flow will \npropagate into the western Conus from the East Pacific during the \nlatter half of the week. However, trends in both operational and \nensemble members suggest the main cold core and negative height \nanomalies even further north than previous iterations. This would \nplace the jet streak only into far NW Arizona while a decaying \ncold front essentially loses definition crossing the coastal range\nresulting in little to no forcing mechanisms into the forecast \narea. Not surprisingly, modest POPs in prior forecasts have mostly\nbeen eliminated. However, forecast confidence is excellent that \nthis trough passage will reinforce temperatures 3F-6F below normal\nthrough the weekend, and potentially result in the first hard \nfreeze of the season for some mid-elevation locations in Gila \nCounty and throughout Joshua Tree NP. Some frost would also be \nlikely in the more rural, sheltered river valleys over the \nweekend. \n\nSubstantial ensemble spread is maintained early next week in a \npattern characterized by northern stream blocking, but a strong \nsouthern stream subtropical jet and progressive flow. GEFS members \nare increasingly trending towards a deepening wave tapping better \nquality moisture over the SW Conus early next week, while the CMC \nand EPS suites are far more disparate and trending towards a lower \namplitude, less impactful system. NBM output seems to be weighted \nmore towards the majority of the EPS/CMC idea with very low POPs \ncentered only over higher terrain areas. However, given the strength \nof the current El Nino episode and intensity of the subtropical jet, \na stronger and more significant weather disturbance cannot be \ndiscounted.",
